  ('8af718c6-ca11-482b-9d6b-832f2b767928', '5003271f-5ac0-433c-95e9-bbaa70f78a40', 'c878e2ec-ad98-4a81-b237-591a32511c85', 'c714d675-9f79-4969-aa3e-70bc893fc2e7', '0fc2b68d-55ca-4916-9321-e27dfb03346c', $qbx$Discuss the concept of 'escape' as it potentially relates to Sam's actions and Charley's initial desires, drawing directly from the provided text.$qbx$, 'long_answer'::question_type, NULL, NULL, 1, 5, ARRAY[]::text[], 'ai_xlsx_import_xlsx_2026-06-08T09-55-16-639Z', 'ba219b8a-c17f-46af-b642-be52f18c80ff', true),
  ('8af718c6-ca11-482b-9d6b-832f2b767928', '5003271f-5ac0-433c-95e9-bbaa70f78a40', 'c878e2ec-ad98-4a81-b237-591a32511c85', 'c714d675-9f79-4969-aa3e-70bc893fc2e7', '0fc2b68d-55ca-4916-9321-e27dfb03346c', $qbx$How does Sam's letter effectively contribute to the overarching theme of an intersection of time and space within the narrative?$qbx$, 'long_answer'::question_type, NULL, NULL, 1, 5, ARRAY[]::text[], 'ai_xlsx_import_xlsx_2026-06-08T09-55-16-639Z', 'ba219b8a-c17f-46af-b642-be52f18c80ff', true),
  ('8af718c6-ca11-482b-9d6b-832f2b767928', '5003271f-5ac0-433c-95e9-bbaa70f78a40', 'c878e2ec-ad98-4a81-b237-591a32511c85', 'c714d675-9f79-4969-aa3e-70bc893fc2e7', '0fc2b68d-55ca-4916-9321-e27dfb03346c', $qbx$Evaluate the statement 'Apparent illogicality sometimes turns out to be a futuristic projection' within the specific context of Sam's discovery and the contents of his letter.$qbx$, 'long_answer'::question_type, NULL, NULL, 1, 5, ARRAY[]::text[], 'ai_xlsx_import_xlsx_2026-06-08T09-55-16-639Z', 'ba219b8a-c17f-46af-b642-be52f18c80ff', true),
  ('8af718c6-ca11-482b-9d6b-832f2b767928', '5003271f-5ac0-433c-95e9-bbaa70f78a40', 'c878e2ec-ad98-4a81-b237-591a32511c85', 'c714d675-9f79-4969-aa3e-70bc893fc2e7', '0fc2b68d-55ca-4916-9321-e27dfb03346c', $qbx$What does the act of purchasing 'eight hundred dollars' worth of old-style currency' reveal about Sam's unwavering commitment to his newly found reality?$qbx$, 'long_answer'::question_type, NULL, NULL, 1, 5, ARRAY[]::text[], 'ai_xlsx_import_xlsx_2026-06-08T09-55-16-639Z', 'ba219b8a-c17f-46af-b642-be52f18c80ff', true),
  ('8af718c6-ca11-482b-9d6b-832f2b767928', '5003271f-5ac0-433c-95e9-bbaa70f78a40', 'c878e2ec-ad98-4a81-b237-591a32511c85', 'c714d675-9f79-4969-aa3e-70bc893fc2e7', '0fc2b68d-55ca-4916-9321-e27dfb03346c', $qbx$Analyze the emotional tone conveyed in Sam's letter and discuss how this tone might significantly influence Charley's perceptions and subsequent actions.$qbx$, 'long_answer'::question_type, NULL, NULL, 1, 5, ARRAY[]::text[], 'ai_xlsx_import_xlsx_2026-06-08T09-55-16-639Z', 'ba219b8a-c17f-46af-b642-be52f18c80ff', true),
  ('8af718c6-ca11-482b-9d6b-832f2b767928', '5003271f-5ac0-433c-95e9-bbaa70f78a40', 'c878e2ec-ad98-4a81-b237-591a32511c85', 'c714d675-9f79-4969-aa3e-70bc893fc2e7', '0fc2b68d-55ca-4916-9321-e27dfb03346c', $qbx$Provide a detailed explanation for why Sam explicitly states that he 'certainly can't go back to his old business' in the setting of 1894 Galesburg.$qbx$, 'long_answer'::question_type, NULL, NULL, 1, 5, ARRAY[]::text[], 'ai_xlsx_import_xlsx_2026-06-08T09-55-16-639Z', 'ba219b8a-c17f-46af-b642-be52f18c80ff', true);
INSERT INTO public.question_bank (id, institution_id, subject_id, topic_id, grade_level_id, class_group_id, question_text, question_type, options, correct_answer, marks, difficulty_level, tags, source, created_by, is_approved, parent_question_id, sub_question_count, sequence_order) VALUES
  ('a7e56eb3-7a7e-477c-8160-ebefd471433b', '8af718c6-ca11-482b-9d6b-832f2b767928', '5003271f-5ac0-433c-95e9-bbaa70f78a40', 'c878e2ec-ad98-4a81-b237-591a32511c85', 'c714d675-9f79-4969-aa3e-70bc893fc2e7', '0fc2b68d-55ca-4916-9321-e27dfb03346c', $qbx$The narrative introduces a man who firmly believes in the existence of a third level at Grand Central Station, a claim vehemently denied by official railroad authorities. His psychiatrist attributes this belief to a 'waking-dream wish fulfillment,' suggesting it's a manifestation of his unhappiness and a deep-seated desire to escape the anxieties of the modern world, characterized by insecurity, fear, war, and worry. This psychological interpretation is supported by his friends but causes distress to his wife. Even his hobby of stamp collecting is re-evaluated through this lens, labeled as a 'temporary refuge from reality,' despite his grandfather, who started the collection, having lived in a seemingly more peaceful era.$qbx$, 'long_answer'::question_type, NULL, NULL, 1, 5, ARRAY[]::text[], 'ai_xlsx_import_xlsx_2026-06-08T09-55-16-639Z', 'ba219b8a-c17f-46af-b642-be52f18c80ff', true, NULL, 17, NULL),
  ('9dd08303-8405-4054-bc9c-08212f88eb3e', '8af718c6-ca11-482b-9d6b-832f2b767928', '5003271f-5ac0-433c-95e9-bbaa70f78a40', 'c878e2ec-ad98-4a81-b237-591a32511c85', 'c714d675-9f79-4969-aa3e-70bc893fc2e7', '0fc2b68d-55ca-4916-9321-e27dfb03346c', $qbx$2 Vistas every U.S. issue, first-day covers, and so on. President Roosevelt collected stamps too, you know. Anyway, here’s what happened at Grand Central. One night last summer I worked late at the office. I was in a hurry to get uptown to my apartment so I decided to take the subway from Grand Central because it’s faster than the bus. Now, I don’t know why this should have happened to me. I’m just an ordinary guy named Charley, thirty-one years old, and I was wearing a tan gabardine suit and a straw hat with a fancy band; I passed a dozen men who looked just like me. And I wasn’t trying to escape from anything; I just wanted to get home to Louisa, my wife. I turned into Grand Central from Vanderbilt Avenue, and went down the steps to the first level, where you take trains like the Twentieth Century. Then I walked down another flight to the second level, where the suburban trains leave from, ducked into an arched doorway heading for the subway — and got lost. That’s easy to do. I’ve been in and out of Grand Central hundreds of times, but I’m always bumping into new doorways and stairs and corridors. Once I got into a tunnel about a mile long and came out in the lobby of the Roosevelt Hotel. Another time I came up in an office building on Forty-sixth Street, three blocks away. Sometimes I think Grand Central is growing like a tree, pushing out new corridors and staircases like roots. There’s probably a long tunnel that nobody knows about feeling its way under the city right now, on its way to Times Square, and maybe another to Central Park. And maybe — because for so many people through the years Reprint 2026-27$qbx$, 'long_answer'::question_type, NULL, NULL, 1, 5, ARRAY[]::text[], 'ai_xlsx_import_xlsx_2026-06-08T09-55-16-639Z', 'ba219b8a-c17f-46af-b642-be52f18c80ff', true, NULL, 3, NULL),
