  ('7e368270-8382-4fec-9613-a3325a8bbd8c', '8af718c6-ca11-482b-9d6b-832f2b767928', '5003271f-5ac0-433c-95e9-bbaa70f78a40', 'c878e2ec-ad98-4a81-b237-591a32511c85', 'c714d675-9f79-4969-aa3e-70bc893fc2e7', '0fc2b68d-55ca-4916-9321-e27dfb03346c', $qbx$2 Vistas every U.S. issue, first-day covers, and so on. President Roosevelt collected stamps too, you know. Anyway, here’s what happened at Grand Central. One night last summer I worked late at the office. I was in a hurry to get uptown to my apartment so I decided to take the subway from Grand Central because it’s faster than the bus. Now, I don’t know why this should have happened to me. I’m just an ordinary guy named Charley, thirty-one years old, and I was wearing a tan gabardine suit and a straw hat with a fancy band; I passed a dozen men who looked just like me. And I wasn’t trying to escape from anything; I just wanted to get home to Louisa, my wife. I turned into Grand Central from Vanderbilt Avenue, and went down the steps to the first level, where you take trains like the Twentieth Century. Then I walked down another flight to the second level, where the suburban trains leave from, ducked into an arched doorway heading for the subway — and got lost. That’s easy to do. I’ve been in and out of Grand Central hundreds of times, but I’m always bumping into new doorways and stairs and corridors. Once I got into a tunnel about a mile long and came out in the lobby of the Roosevelt Hotel. Another time I came up in an office building on Forty-sixth Street, three blocks away. Sometimes I think Grand Central is growing like a tree, pushing out new corridors and staircases like roots. There’s probably a long tunnel that nobody knows about feeling its way under the city right now, on its way to Times Square, and maybe another to Central Park. And maybe — because for so many people through the years Reprint 2026-27$qbx$, 'long_answer'::question_type, NULL, NULL, 1, 5, ARRAY[]::text[], 'ai_xlsx_import_xlsx_2026-06-08T09-55-16-639Z', 'ba219b8a-c17f-46af-b642-be52f18c80ff', true, NULL, 2, NULL),
  ('b20998f1-7bd9-4f2c-adc2-ada818d8321c', '8af718c6-ca11-482b-9d6b-832f2b767928', '5003271f-5ac0-433c-95e9-bbaa70f78a40', 'c878e2ec-ad98-4a81-b237-591a32511c85', 'c714d675-9f79-4969-aa3e-70bc893fc2e7', '0fc2b68d-55ca-4916-9321-e27dfb03346c', $qbx$Grand Central has been an exit, a way of escape — maybe that’s how the tunnel I got into... But I never told my psychiatrist friend about that idea.$qbx$, 'long_answer'::question_type, NULL, NULL, 1, 5, ARRAY[]::text[], 'ai_xlsx_import_xlsx_2026-06-08T09-55-16-639Z', 'ba219b8a-c17f-46af-b642-be52f18c80ff', true, NULL, 3, NULL),
  ('37ed711c-e357-4f4d-a495-15308e60ee37', '8af718c6-ca11-482b-9d6b-832f2b767928', '5003271f-5ac0-433c-95e9-bbaa70f78a40', 'c878e2ec-ad98-4a81-b237-591a32511c85', 'c714d675-9f79-4969-aa3e-70bc893fc2e7', '0fc2b68d-55ca-4916-9321-e27dfb03346c', $qbx$The corridor I was in began angling left and slanting downward and I thought that was wrong, but I kept on walking. All I could hear was the empty sound of my own footsteps and I didn’t pass a soul.$qbx$, 'long_answer'::question_type, NULL, NULL, 1, 5, ARRAY[]::text[], 'ai_xlsx_import_xlsx_2026-06-08T09-55-16-639Z', 'ba219b8a-c17f-46af-b642-be52f18c80ff', true, NULL, 3, NULL),
  ('4b0d9d3f-ce47-4fd6-8a87-ba63dfe78d90', '8af718c6-ca11-482b-9d6b-832f2b767928', '5003271f-5ac0-433c-95e9-bbaa70f78a40', 'c878e2ec-ad98-4a81-b237-591a32511c85', 'c714d675-9f79-4969-aa3e-70bc893fc2e7', '0fc2b68d-55ca-4916-9321-e27dfb03346c', $qbx$Then I heard that sort of hollow roar ahead that means open space and people talking. The tunnel turned sharp left; I went down a short flight of stairs and came out on the third level at Grand Central Station.$qbx$, 'long_answer'::question_type, NULL, NULL, 1, 5, ARRAY[]::text[], 'ai_xlsx_import_xlsx_2026-06-08T09-55-16-639Z', 'ba219b8a-c17f-46af-b642-be52f18c80ff', true, NULL, 3, NULL),
  ('e3774937-94e5-48d8-8336-37d755bcabe3', '8af718c6-ca11-482b-9d6b-832f2b767928', '5003271f-5ac0-433c-95e9-bbaa70f78a40', 'c878e2ec-ad98-4a81-b237-591a32511c85', 'c714d675-9f79-4969-aa3e-70bc893fc2e7', '0fc2b68d-55ca-4916-9321-e27dfb03346c', $qbx$For just a moment I thought I was back on the second level, but I saw the room was smaller, there were fewer ticket windows and train gates, and the information booth in the centre was wood and old-looking. And the man in the booth wore a green eyeshade and long black sleeve protectors.$qbx$, 'long_answer'::question_type, NULL, NULL, 1, 5, ARRAY[]::text[], 'ai_xlsx_import_xlsx_2026-06-08T09-55-16-639Z', 'ba219b8a-c17f-46af-b642-be52f18c80ff', true, NULL, 3, NULL),
  ('63e4b00f-96e1-44f1-897a-0de233faf34b', '8af718c6-ca11-482b-9d6b-832f2b767928', '5003271f-5ac0-433c-95e9-bbaa70f78a40', 'c878e2ec-ad98-4a81-b237-591a32511c85', 'c714d675-9f79-4969-aa3e-70bc893fc2e7', '0fc2b68d-55ca-4916-9321-e27dfb03346c', $qbx$The lights were dim and sort of flickering. Then I saw why; they were open-flame gaslights. There were brass spittoons on the floor, and across the station a glint of light caught my eye; a man was pulling a gold watch from his vest pocket. He snapped open the cover, glanced at his watch and frowned.$qbx$, 'long_answer'::question_type, NULL, NULL, 1, 5, ARRAY[]::text[], 'ai_xlsx_import_xlsx_2026-06-08T09-55-16-639Z', 'ba219b8a-c17f-46af-b642-be52f18c80ff', true, NULL, 3, NULL),
  ('a9bac3bb-6d7b-4d19-af76-fbcf02ffa805', '8af718c6-ca11-482b-9d6b-832f2b767928', '5003271f-5ac0-433c-95e9-bbaa70f78a40', 'c878e2ec-ad98-4a81-b237-591a32511c85', 'c714d675-9f79-4969-aa3e-70bc893fc2e7', '0fc2b68d-55ca-4916-9321-e27dfb03346c', $qbx$He wore a derby hat, a black four-button suit with tiny lapels, and he had a big, black, handlebar m u st ache. Then I looked around and saw that everyone in the station was dressed like eighteen-ninety-something; I never saw so Reprint 2026-27$qbx$, 'long_answer'::question_type, NULL, NULL, 1, 5, ARRAY[]::text[], 'ai_xlsx_import_xlsx_2026-06-08T09-55-16-639Z', 'ba219b8a-c17f-46af-b642-be52f18c80ff', true, NULL, 3, NULL),
  ('38668322-57b4-4515-812b-0f0eb21a694e', '8af718c6-ca11-482b-9d6b-832f2b767928', '5003271f-5ac0-433c-95e9-bbaa70f78a40', 'c878e2ec-ad98-4a81-b237-591a32511c85', 'c714d675-9f79-4969-aa3e-70bc893fc2e7', '0fc2b68d-55ca-4916-9321-e27dfb03346c', $qbx$Grand Central has been an exit, a way of escape — maybe that’s how the tunnel I got into...$qbx$, 'long_answer'::question_type, NULL, NULL, 1, 5, ARRAY[]::text[], 'ai_xlsx_import_xlsx_2026-06-08T09-55-16-639Z', 'ba219b8a-c17f-46af-b642-be52f18c80ff', true, NULL, 3, NULL),
  ('83adff01-de5b-4027-b2fa-379e4ac01109', '8af718c6-ca11-482b-9d6b-832f2b767928', '5003271f-5ac0-433c-95e9-bbaa70f78a40', 'c878e2ec-ad98-4a81-b237-591a32511c85', 'c714d675-9f79-4969-aa3e-70bc893fc2e7', '0fc2b68d-55ca-4916-9321-e27dfb03346c', $qbx$But I never told my psychiatrist friend about that idea.$qbx$, 'long_answer'::question_type, NULL, NULL, 1, 5, ARRAY[]::text[], 'ai_xlsx_import_xlsx_2026-06-08T09-55-16-639Z', 'ba219b8a-c17f-46af-b642-be52f18c80ff', true, NULL, 3, NULL),
  ('1758533e-9d49-4bd4-be86-de49ee76e7c3', '8af718c6-ca11-482b-9d6b-832f2b767928', '5003271f-5ac0-433c-95e9-bbaa70f78a40', 'c878e2ec-ad98-4a81-b237-591a32511c85', 'c714d675-9f79-4969-aa3e-70bc893fc2e7', '0fc2b68d-55ca-4916-9321-e27dfb03346c', $qbx$The corridor I was in began angling left and slanting downward and I thought that was wrong, but I kept on walking.$qbx$, 'long_answer'::question_type, NULL, NULL, 1, 5, ARRAY[]::text[], 'ai_xlsx_import_xlsx_2026-06-08T09-55-16-639Z', 'ba219b8a-c17f-46af-b642-be52f18c80ff', true, NULL, 3, NULL),
  ('c08c6955-c1d8-474e-b419-ed321d4260ee', '8af718c6-ca11-482b-9d6b-832f2b767928', '5003271f-5ac0-433c-95e9-bbaa70f78a40', 'c878e2ec-ad98-4a81-b237-591a32511c85', 'c714d675-9f79-4969-aa3e-70bc893fc2e7', '0fc2b68d-55ca-4916-9321-e27dfb03346c', $qbx$All I could hear was the empty sound of my own footsteps and I didn’t pass a soul.$qbx$, 'long_answer'::question_type, NULL, NULL, 1, 5, ARRAY[]::text[], 'ai_xlsx_import_xlsx_2026-06-08T09-55-16-639Z', 'ba219b8a-c17f-46af-b642-be52f18c80ff', true, NULL, 3, NULL),
  ('35ffc40a-b636-46f7-b901-daf5b4a15354', '8af718c6-ca11-482b-9d6b-832f2b767928', '5003271f-5ac0-433c-95e9-bbaa70f78a40', 'c878e2ec-ad98-4a81-b237-591a32511c85', 'c714d675-9f79-4969-aa3e-70bc893fc2e7', '0fc2b68d-55ca-4916-9321-e27dfb03346c', $qbx$Then I heard that sort of hollow roar ahead that means open space and people talking. The tunnel turned sharp left; I went down a short flight of stairs and came out on the third level at Grand Central Station.$qbx$, 'long_answer'::question_type, NULL, NULL, 1, 5, ARRAY[]::text[], 'ai_xlsx_import_xlsx_2026-06-08T09-55-16-639Z', 'ba219b8a-c17f-46af-b642-be52f18c80ff', true, NULL, 3, NULL),
  ('dd8ce804-3b3b-46b8-a5a0-886db4ca9a3f', '8af718c6-ca11-482b-9d6b-832f2b767928', '5003271f-5ac0-433c-95e9-bbaa70f78a40', 'c878e2ec-ad98-4a81-b237-591a32511c85', 'c714d675-9f79-4969-aa3e-70bc893fc2e7', '0fc2b68d-55ca-4916-9321-e27dfb03346c', $qbx$For just a moment I thought I was back on the second level, but I saw the room was smaller, there were fewer ticket windows and train gates, and the information booth in the centre was wood and old-looking.$qbx$, 'long_answer'::question_type, NULL, NULL, 1, 5, ARRAY[]::text[], 'ai_xlsx_import_xlsx_2026-06-08T09-55-16-639Z', 'ba219b8a-c17f-46af-b642-be52f18c80ff', true, NULL, 3, NULL),
  ('f4ad6570-e93d-435b-a397-59ea3e91925a', '8af718c6-ca11-482b-9d6b-832f2b767928', '5003271f-5ac0-433c-95e9-bbaa70f78a40', 'c878e2ec-ad98-4a81-b237-591a32511c85', 'c714d675-9f79-4969-aa3e-70bc893fc2e7', '0fc2b68d-55ca-4916-9321-e27dfb03346c', $qbx$And the man in the booth wore a green eyeshade and long black sleeve protectors. The lights were dim and sort of flickering. Then I saw why; they were open-flame gaslights.$qbx$, 'long_answer'::question_type, NULL, NULL, 1, 5, ARRAY[]::text[], 'ai_xlsx_import_xlsx_2026-06-08T09-55-16-639Z', 'ba219b8a-c17f-46af-b642-be52f18c80ff', true, NULL, 3, NULL),
  ('cb85531f-5f97-40f7-a09e-dcf45f179bd0', '8af718c6-ca11-482b-9d6b-832f2b767928', '5003271f-5ac0-433c-95e9-bbaa70f78a40', 'c878e2ec-ad98-4a81-b237-591a32511c85', 'c714d675-9f79-4969-aa3e-70bc893fc2e7', '0fc2b68d-55ca-4916-9321-e27dfb03346c', $qbx$There were brass spittoons on the floor, and across the station a glint of light caught my eye; a man was pulling a gold watch from his vest pocket. He snapped open the cover, glanced at his watch and frowned.$qbx$, 'long_answer'::question_type, NULL, NULL, 1, 5, ARRAY[]::text[], 'ai_xlsx_import_xlsx_2026-06-08T09-55-16-639Z', 'ba219b8a-c17f-46af-b642-be52f18c80ff', true, NULL, 3, NULL),
  ('bdd68e3d-d67c-4beb-9957-e747893e234d', '8af718c6-ca11-482b-9d6b-832f2b767928', '5003271f-5ac0-433c-95e9-bbaa70f78a40', 'c878e2ec-ad98-4a81-b237-591a32511c85', 'c714d675-9f79-4969-aa3e-70bc893fc2e7', '0fc2b68d-55ca-4916-9321-e27dfb03346c', $qbx$He wore a derby hat, a black four-button suit with tiny lapels, and he had a big, black, handlebar m u st ache.$qbx$, 'long_answer'::question_type, NULL, NULL, 1, 5, ARRAY[]::text[], 'ai_xlsx_import_xlsx_2026-06-08T09-55-16-639Z', 'ba219b8a-c17f-46af-b642-be52f18c80ff', true, NULL, 3, NULL),
  ('ee79f7da-223a-4068-9224-4073add24fdb', '8af718c6-ca11-482b-9d6b-832f2b767928', '5003271f-5ac0-433c-95e9-bbaa70f78a40', 'c878e2ec-ad98-4a81-b237-591a32511c85', 'c714d675-9f79-4969-aa3e-70bc893fc2e7', '0fc2b68d-55ca-4916-9321-e27dfb03346c', $qbx$Then I looked around and saw that everyone in the station was dressed like eighteen-ninety-something; I never saw so Reprint 2026-27$qbx$, 'long_answer'::question_type, NULL, NULL, 1, 5, ARRAY[]::text[], 'ai_xlsx_import_xlsx_2026-06-08T09-55-16-639Z', 'ba219b8a-c17f-46af-b642-be52f18c80ff', true, NULL, 3, NULL),
  ('671e74df-46d4-407f-979f-b553188d9abc', '8af718c6-ca11-482b-9d6b-832f2b767928', '5003271f-5ac0-433c-95e9-bbaa70f78a40', 'c878e2ec-ad98-4a81-b237-591a32511c85', 'c714d675-9f79-4969-aa3e-70bc893fc2e7', '0fc2b68d-55ca-4916-9321-e27dfb03346c', $qbx$The Third Level Grand Central has been an exit, a way of escape — maybe that’s how the tunnel I got into... But I never told my psychiatrist friend about that idea. The corridor I was in began angling left and slanting downward and I thought that was wrong, but I kept on walking. All I could hear was the empty sound of my own footsteps and I didn’t pass a soul. Then I heard that sort of hollow roar ahead that means open space and people talking. The tunnel turned sharp left; I went down a short flight of stairs and came out on the third level at Grand Central Station. For just a moment I thought I was back on the second level, but I saw the room was smaller, there were fewer ticket windows and train gates, and the information booth in the centre was wood and old-looking. And the man in the booth wore a green eyeshade and long black sleeve protectors. The lights were dim and sort of flickering. Then I saw why; they were open-flame gaslights. There were brass spittoons on the floor, and across the station a glint of light caught my eye; a man was pulling a gold watch from his vest pocket. He snapped open the cover, glanced at his watch and frowned. He wore a derby hat, a black four-button suit with tiny lapels, and he had a big, black, handlebar m u st ache. Then I looked around and saw that everyone in the station was dressed like eighteen-ninety-something; I never saw so Reprint 2026-27$qbx$, 'long_answer'::question_type, NULL, NULL, 1, 5, ARRAY[]::text[], 'ai_xlsx_import_xlsx_2026-06-08T09-55-16-639Z', 'ba219b8a-c17f-46af-b642-be52f18c80ff', true, NULL, 3, NULL),
  ('7936be0e-4b6c-407c-b61b-3d4711dd83e2', '8af718c6-ca11-482b-9d6b-832f2b767928', '5003271f-5ac0-433c-95e9-bbaa70f78a40', 'c878e2ec-ad98-4a81-b237-591a32511c85', 'c714d675-9f79-4969-aa3e-70bc893fc2e7', '0fc2b68d-55ca-4916-9321-e27dfb03346c', $qbx$4 Vistas many beards, sideburns and fancy mustaches in my life. A woman walked in through the train gate; she wore a dress with leg-of- mutton sleeves and skirts to the top of her high-buttoned shoes. Back of her, out on the tracks, I caught a glimpse of a locomotive, a very small Currier & Ives locomotive with a funnel-shaped stack. And then I knew. To make sure, I walked over to a newsboy and glanced at the stack of papers at his feet. It was The World; and The World hasn’t been published for years. The lead story said something about President Cleveland. I’ve found that front page since, in the Public Library files, and it was printed June 11, 1894. I turned toward the ticket windows knowing that here — on the third level at Grand Central — I could buy tickets that would take Louisa and me anywhere in the United States we wanted to go. In the year 1894. And I wanted two tickets to Galesburg, Illinois. Have you ever been there? It’s a wonderful town still, with big old frame houses, huge lawns, and tremendous trees whose branches meet overhead and roof the streets. And in 1894, summer evenings were twice as long, and people sat out on their lawns, the men smoking cigars and talking quietly, the women waving palm- leaf fans, with the fire-flies all around, in a peaceful world. To be back there with the First World War still twenty years off, and World War II over forty years in the future... I wanted two tickets for that. The clerk figured the fare — he glanced at my fancy hatband, but he figured the fare — and I had enough for two coach tickets, one way. But when I counted out the money and looked up, the clerk was staring at me. He nodded at the bills. ‘‘That ain’t money, mister,’’ he said, ‘‘and if you’re trying to skin me, you won’t get very far,’’ and he glanced at the cash drawer beside him. Of course the money was old-style bills, half again as big as the money we use nowadays, and different-looking. I turned away and got out fast. There’s nothing nice about jail, even in 1894. Reprint 2026-27$qbx$, 'long_answer'::question_type, NULL, NULL, 1, 5, ARRAY[]::text[], 'ai_xlsx_import_xlsx_2026-06-08T09-55-16-639Z', 'ba219b8a-c17f-46af-b642-be52f18c80ff', true, NULL, 2, NULL),
