-639Z', 'ba219b8a-c17f-46af-b642-be52f18c80ff', true, NULL, 3, NULL),
  ('ffce521d-0091-4ce2-9a8e-5aade0a21fcf', '8af718c6-ca11-482b-9d6b-832f2b767928', '5003271f-5ac0-433c-95e9-bbaa70f78a40', 'c878e2ec-ad98-4a81-b237-591a32511c85', 'c714d675-9f79-4969-aa3e-70bc893fc2e7', '0fc2b68d-55ca-4916-9321-e27dfb03346c', $qbx$The Third Level Grand Central has been an exit, a way of escape — maybe that’s how the tunnel I got into... But I never told my psychiatrist friend about that idea. The corridor I was in began angling left and slanting downward and I thought that was wrong, but I kept on walking. All I could hear was the empty sound of my own footsteps and I didn’t pass a soul. Then I heard that sort of hollow roar ahead that means open space and people talking. The tunnel turned sharp left; I went down a short flight of stairs and came out on the third level at Grand Central Station. For just a moment I thought I was back on the second level, but I saw the room was smaller, there were fewer ticket windows and train gates, and the information booth in the centre was wood and old-looking. And the man in the booth wore a green eyeshade and long black sleeve protectors. The lights were dim and sort of flickering. Then I saw why; they were open-flame gaslights. There were brass spittoons on the floor, and across the station a glint of light caught my eye; a man was pulling a gold watch from his vest pocket. He snapped open the cover, glanced at his watch and frowned. He wore a derby hat, a black four-button suit with tiny lapels, and he had a big, black, handlebar m u st ache. Then I looked around and saw that everyone in the station was dressed like eighteen-ninety-something; I never saw so Reprint 2026-27$qbx$, 'comprehension'::question_type, NULL, NULL, 1, 5, ARRAY[]::text[], 'ai_xlsx_import_xlsx_2026-06-08T09-55-16-639Z', 'ba219b8a-c17f-46af-b642-be52f18c80ff', true, NULL, 3, NULL),
  ('570fd7ce-ed24-464e-9da7-fd6755c8b006', '8af718c6-ca11-482b-9d6b-832f2b767928', '5003271f-5ac0-433c-95e9-bbaa70f78a40', 'c878e2ec-ad98-4a81-b237-591a32511c85', 'c714d675-9f79-4969-aa3e-70bc893fc2e7', '0fc2b68d-55ca-4916-9321-e27dfb03346c', $qbx$The Third Level Grand Central has been an exit, a way of escape — maybe that’s how the tunnel I got into... But I never told my psychiatrist friend about that idea. The corridor I was in began angling left and slanting downward and I thought that was wrong, but I kept on walking. All I could hear was the empty sound of my own footsteps and I didn’t pass a soul. Then I heard that sort of hollow roar ahead that means open space and people talking. The tunnel turned sharp left; I went down a short flight of stairs and came out on the third level at Grand Central Station. For just a moment I thought I was back on the second level, but I saw the room was smaller, there were fewer ticket windows and train gates, and the information booth in the centre was wood and old-looking. And the man in the booth wore a green eyeshade and long black sleeve protectors. The lights were dim and sort of flickering. Then I saw why; they were open-flame gaslights. There were brass spittoons on the floor, and across the station a glint of light caught my eye; a man was pulling a gold watch from his vest pocket. He snapped open the cover, glanced at his watch and frowned. He wore a derby hat, a black four-button suit with tiny lapels, and he had a big, black, handlebar m u st ache. Then I looked around and saw that everyone in the station was dressed like eighteen-ninety-something; I never saw so Reprint 2026-27$qbx$, 'comprehension'::question_type, NULL, NULL, 1, 5, ARRAY[]::text[], 'ai_xlsx_import_xlsx_2026-06-08T09-55-16-639Z', 'ba219b8a-c17f-46af-b642-be52f18c80ff', true, NULL, 3, NULL),
